        "fr-extract.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "466dd7e9-8930-3abd-b1bb-4c50cf0545d7",
                "word": "indolence",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "fr",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "6719de49-30b1-4e1a-a46f-088dc388864a",
                        "entry_id": "466dd7e9-8930-3abd-b1bb-4c50cf0545d7",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "indolences"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "22512ef9-3fdd-3ebe-ba2a-c478d4978a7c",
                        "entry_id": "466dd7e9-8930-3abd-b1bb-4c50cf0545d7",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Disposition à éviter tout effort, toute peine."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "7f81fb81-b5e4-47dc-a70a-17bad092f055",
                                "sense_id": "22512ef9-3fdd-3ebe-ba2a-c478d4978a7c",
                                "text": "Appuyée sur le dos du siège et légèrement inclinée, la tête de ce brave père restait dans une pose dont l’indolence peignait un calme parfait, un doux épanouissement de joie."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "02847878-7f63-4cf0-9711-00537e9af640",
                                "sense_id": "22512ef9-3fdd-3ebe-ba2a-c478d4978a7c",
                                "text": "Le ciel trop doux, le soleil trop resplendissant, l’air où traînait comme un souffle de langueur, qui invitait à l’indolence et à la volupté très lente, […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "0ff5e5db-e27a-42d5-87a8-171a0ce5b0fd",
                                "sense_id": "22512ef9-3fdd-3ebe-ba2a-c478d4978a7c",
                                "text": "On aurait pu se croire dans un fumoir anglais\nNous sirotions à deux un breuvage irlandais\nQui donnait à chacun une diction pâteuse\nUne douce apathie, une indolence heureuse."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "1689f476-827b-423e-a461-08e183206dff",
                        "source_entry_id": "466dd7e9-8930-3abd-b1bb-4c50cf0545d7",
                        "source_sense_id": "22512ef9-3fdd-3ebe-ba2a-c478d4978a7c",
                        "source_sense_description": "Disposition à éviter tout effort, toute peine",
                        "target_lang_word": "Indolence",
                        "target_lang_code": "de",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": []
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "bb161f3c-00ba-4823-8fa9-ca5b719ce7d1",
                        "source_entry_id": "466dd7e9-8930-3abd-b1bb-4c50cf0545d7",
                        "source_sense_id": "22512ef9-3fdd-3ebe-ba2a-c478d4978a7c",
                        "source_sense_description": "Disposition à éviter tout effort, toute peine",
                        "target_lang_word": "indolence",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "indolence",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "32a15a03-2c79-34c2-a31d-18f1f47fdf5e",
                                        "entry_id": "5f2eba30-5d4f-3663-8893-2440a5204477",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Habitual laziness or sloth."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable",
                                            "usually"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "5a9c83d1-6846-4c2d-8072-2f21452d70c4",
                                                "sense_id": "32a15a03-2c79-34c2-a31d-18f1f47fdf5e",
                                                "text": "After having applied my mind with more than ordinary attention to my studies, it is my usual custom to relax and unbend it in the conversation of such as are rather easy than shining companions. […] This is the particular use I make of a set of heavy honest men, with whom I have passed many hours with much indolence, though not with great pleasure. Their conversation is a kind of preparative for sleep: […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "6e8e9db2-99b6-4a8a-940a-6528c723f42a",
                                                "sense_id": "32a15a03-2c79-34c2-a31d-18f1f47fdf5e",
                                                "text": "The sacred indolence of the monks was devoutly embraced by a servile and effeminate age; but if superstition had not afforded a decent retreat, the same vices would have tempted the unworthy Romans to desert, from baser motives, the standard of the republic."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "07fe2169-66b0-46d4-916c-162244509128",
                                                "sense_id": "32a15a03-2c79-34c2-a31d-18f1f47fdf5e",
                                                "text": "He [Samuel Johnson] ſeemed to learn by intuition; for though indolence and procraſtination vvere inherent in his conſtitution, vvhenever he made an exertion he did more than any one elſe."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c09cb926-4105-4226-918d-c4c7cf4f9c3f",
                                                "sense_id": "32a15a03-2c79-34c2-a31d-18f1f47fdf5e",
                                                "text": "It is indolence Mr. Bertram, indeed. Indolence and love of ease—a want of all laudable ambition, of taste for good company, or of inclination to take the trouble of being agreeable, which make men Clergymen."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0efe094d-16a5-4201-b0f7-99b0294e5c05",
                                                "sense_id": "32a15a03-2c79-34c2-a31d-18f1f47fdf5e",
                                                "text": "To the man himself [Samuel Taylor Coleridge] Nature had given, in high measure, the seeds of a noble endowment; […] but imbedded in such weak laxity of character, in such indolences and esuriences as had made strange work with it."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ecd455e9-038a-4ef9-869d-90ee610d1824",
                                                "sense_id": "32a15a03-2c79-34c2-a31d-18f1f47fdf5e",
                                                "text": "Heedless of the rights of fellows and founders' bequests, of sleepy dignities and established indolences, they re-established long-dormant lectures in the colleges."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "9671dc22-6039-49a3-ab43-7bc5cd0136ca",
                                                "sense_id": "32a15a03-2c79-34c2-a31d-18f1f47fdf5e",
                                                "text": "[T]here is an incessant and ever-flowing current of human affairs towards the worse, consisting of all the follies, all the vices, all the negligences, indolences, and supinenesses of mankind; which is only controlled, and kept from sweeping all before it, by the exertions which some persons constantly, and others by fits, put forth in the direction of good and worthy objects."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "fb9dc8dc-602a-4067-81cd-bbd372fcc2ec",
                                                "sense_id": "32a15a03-2c79-34c2-a31d-18f1f47fdf5e",
                                                "text": "There are others who have let their reputations become incrusted or blighted with indolences, or self-indulgence, or errors that are not errors unto death. These should not think to put away the tarnished name; but rather, by some new life and better efforts, to raise it from ignominy until it shows clean again."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "854d1f34-0a18-4feb-9ca5-991655b25b07",
                                                "sense_id": "32a15a03-2c79-34c2-a31d-18f1f47fdf5e",
                                                "text": "Her cheeks were glowing, and her whole figure expressed a tense vibrant life in singular contrast to the apparent indolence of the men at whom she was talking."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1986a9f2-fd80-47b8-8d77-e768c4c284d7",
                                                "sense_id": "32a15a03-2c79-34c2-a31d-18f1f47fdf5e",
                                                "text": "[N]ow, after five weeks of doing nothing, I am an authority on the subject of indolence and glad to share my views with you."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "538fc3dc-60f2-37b5-b995-9de8ddc9df54",
                                        "entry_id": "5f2eba30-5d4f-3663-8893-2440a5204477",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Lack of pain in a tumour."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable",
                                            "usually"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "863130d2-4c11-370d-af56-667119d22d4b",
                                        "entry_id": "5f2eba30-5d4f-3663-8893-2440a5204477",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A state in which one feels no pain or is indifferent to it; a lack of any feeling."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "obsolete",
                                            "uncountable",
                                            "usually"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "794bed59-46f6-4538-be57-f16485b16ba5",
                                                "sense_id": "863130d2-4c11-370d-af56-667119d22d4b",
                                                "text": "Novv, to begin vvith Fortitude, they ſay it is the meane betvveen Covvardiſe & raſh Audacitie, […] Clemencie & Mildneſſe, betvveene ſenſeleſſe Indolence and Crueltie: […]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "1a05a1db-bef9-3c00-b2d7-3e62e5868b7d",
                                        "entry_id": "5f2eba30-5d4f-3663-8893-2440a5204477",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A state of repose in which neither pain nor pleasure is experienced."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "obsolete",
                                            "uncountable",
                                            "usually"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "069336d8-bb81-4ee5-96e9-7f7b5f4a8fcc",
                                                "sense_id": "1a05a1db-bef9-3c00-b2d7-3e62e5868b7d",
                                                "text": "Indolence, vvhich Epicure held, they eſteem not pleaſure, nor vvant of pleaſure, griefe, for both theſe conſiſt in motion; but Indolence and vvant of pleaſure conſiſts not in motion, for Indolence is like the ſtate of a ſleeping man."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "2b0ca714-5d37-4158-bff3-f4136b5bfc07",
                        "source_entry_id": "466dd7e9-8930-3abd-b1bb-4c50cf0545d7",
